<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Singhana II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 1210–<a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Singhana II was the most powerful king of the Seuna (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a>) dynasty of Devagiri in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He extended Seuna rule over most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> at the expense of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and other rivals. His court patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning, including the musicologist Sharngadeva and the astronomer Changadeva.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/singhana-ii.jpg" alt="Singhana II" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_virgal_(herostone)_in_Kaitabhesvara_temple_at_Kubatur.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Singhana was the son of his predecessor Jaitugi. His birth was believed to have come from the blessing of the goddess Narasimhi of Parnakheta, after whom he was named. While still a prince he appears to have taken part in his father's campaign against the Kakatiyas, in which the Kakatiya king Mahadeva was killed and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganapati-kakatiya">Ganapati</a> was installed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> vassal. Ganapati, who had spent his early life in Yadava captivity, kept cordial relations with Singhana, and no major conflict arose between them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>To avenge his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhillama-v">Bhillama V</a>'s defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Ballala II at Soratur, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a> pushed south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaprabha-river">Malaprabha River</a>, reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anantapur">Anantapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballari">Bellary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a> and Shimoga districts by 1212 and the land north of the Tungabhadra by 1220, helped by the Rattas of Saundatti. In 1216 Singhana marched against the Shilahara king Bhoja II, who fled to Panhala, and annexed his kingdom with Kolhapur. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1215; the court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemadri">Hemadri</a>'s claim that the Paramara king Arjunavarman died is doubted.</p><p>In Lata his general Kholeshvara defeated and killed the Chahamana ruler Simha in 1220, and Simha's nephew Shankha was released to rule as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> feudatory. A joint invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> collapsed when the Chaulukya general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavanaprasada">Lavanaprasada</a> sowed distrust among the allies, according to legends of a stolen horse and a forged letter, and the conflict ended with a peace treaty around 1232. In the south the general Bichana conquered the Ratta principality and subdued the Guttas and Kadambas, while a 30,000-strong cavalry took the Gutti fort after a rebellion in 1237. Many inscriptions credit Singhana with conquests across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that historians regard as conventional poetic claims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son Jaitugi II, heir apparent in 1229, probably died before him, so Singhana was succeeded by his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>; historians differ on the exact year. Singhana's court included the astronomers Anantadeva, who wrote commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmagupta">Brahmagupta</a> and Varahamihira, and Changadeva, who founded a college of astronomy at Patana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a> in memory of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaskara-ii">Bhaskaracharya</a>. A commentary on Sharngadeva's Sangita-ratnakara is attributed to Singhana himself, though he may only have been its patron. The long wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> weakened both kingdoms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simhana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Singhana II</b>, the greatest ruler, extended Seuna power over most of Maharashtra and northern Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/state/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Bhoja II, the last major king, asserted independence as Chalukya power collapsed, but around 1212 the Yadava king <b>Singhana</b> conquered Kolhapur and ended the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/silaharas-of-kolhapur">Silaharas of Kolhapur</a></li></ul></section>
